class InjectionToken<T> { 
 constructor(public value: T) {}
}
class Injector {
 private readonly dependencies = new Map<
 InjectionToken<any>, any
 >(); 
 provide<T>(dependency: T): InjectionToken<T> { 
 const token = new InjectionToken(dependency);
 this.dependencies.set(token, dependency);
 return token;
 }
 inject<T>(token: InjectionToken<T>): T { 
 const dependency = this.dependencies.get(token) as T;
 return dependency;
 }
 
Angular creates special injectors when our application runs, provides dependencies, and then injects them into components (directives, pipes, etc.) implicitly. 
Any Angular application has at least one injector, known as the root injector, which is globally created for our application at the very beginning. The services marked with providedIn: 'root' are provided here
